# Python simplify

A whole-codebase sweep for **accidental complexity** — code that is harder to
read than the problem requires. This is not a bug hunt (`/code-review` does
that) and not a diff review (`/simplify` does that). It reads modules nobody
has touched recently, on the theory that unchanged code is exactly where
complexity accretes unobserved.

The deliverable is **a phased plan another session can execute without making
design decisions**, not a findings list. A findings list gets skimmed; a plan
with one commit per phase, a named stop rule, and the counter-argument recorded
next to each fix gets done.

Three properties make the output worth more than a generic "clean this up" pass:

1. **Every module gets a verdict.** Act, park, or fine — including the ones you
   found nothing in. Without an explicit park list the next run re-reviews the
   whole tree and the exercise never converges.
2. **Phases are ordered by behaviour risk, not by dependency.** Everything that
   provably cannot change observable behaviour goes first, so the suite is
   proven green before anything that can.
3. **Each finding carries its rejected alternative.** The reason a fix stopped
   where it did is the part that decays fastest and costs the most to
   rediscover. Write it down or a later session undoes the reasoning.

## 0. Mode

**The default is a full sweep.** Bare invocation, or anything that isn't one of
the narrower modes below, runs §1–§5 across the whole tree.

| Invocation | Mode |
|---|---|
| bare, or anything unrecognized | **full sweep** — §1–§5 over the whole tree |
| a path or module name is named | **scoped sweep** — §1–§5 over that subtree only |
| `--sweep` / "just tell me what's there" | **sweep only** — §1–§3, stop before writing the plan |
| `--execute <phase>` / "do phase B" | **execute** (§6) one phase of an existing plan |
| `--fix <id>` / "fix B2" | **fix** (§6) one finding of an existing plan |
| `--promote` / "put it in the specs" | **promote** (§5.3) an existing review into the design record |

### 0.1 A sweep builds on the last sweep

Before anything else, read the output directory §5.1 resolves — `context.md`
(§5.2) for where things live, and the newest `complexity-review-*.md` for what
was already decided. Then, in the new review:

- Findings marked **done** stay done. Do not re-derive a fix that has already
  shipped; carry the line forward with its date so the history survives.
- Modules marked **park** or **fine** are **not** re-read unless `git log` shows
  the file changed since that review's date. Carry their verdict forward and say
  in the new review that you did.
- Findings marked **open** are re-checked against the current code — the fix may
  have become unnecessary, or the module may have moved — and then carried
  forward, keeping their original id.

Carry ids forward unchanged. An id that meant one thing in the March review and
something else in the June one makes `--fix B2` ambiguous and makes the two
documents impossible to read together.

**A full clean sweep is: delete the output directory's contents and run again.**
That is the supported reset — there is no `--force` flag, and nothing outside
that directory needs undoing, which is exactly why the review does not write
into the design record on its own (§5.3).

## 1. Orient before reading code

Cheap, and it changes what counts as a finding.

- **Repo conventions** — `CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`, `.cursorrules`, contributing
  docs. These frequently record deliberate trade-offs. A "finding" that a
  documented decision already covers is noise, and proposing it burns the
  user's trust in the rest of the list.
- **The design record**, if there is one. Look for `docs/specs/`, `docs/adr/`,
  `docs/architecture/`, `doc/`, `documentation/`, `rfcs/`, and whatever
  `CLAUDE.md`/`AGENTS.md`/`README.md` point at — repos put it in more places
  than the conventional three. Prior phases tell you what was already
  refactored and what was deliberately parked. If discovery finds **nothing**,
  or finds two plausible candidates and no way to choose, ask the user where it
  lives rather than guessing{% if agent == "claude" %} (`AskUserQuestion`, offering the candidates
  you found plus "no design record"){% endif %}; record the answer in `context.md` (§5.2) so no
  later run asks again.
- **The command vocabulary** — `Taskfile.yml`, `Makefile`, `noxfile.py`,
  `tox.ini`, `pyproject.toml` `[tool.*]`. Whatever the repo uses to run
  lint/typecheck/test is what you will use. **Never invoke `pytest`, `ruff`,
  `mypy` or `uv` directly if the repo has a wrapper** — several repos enforce
  this with a CI linter.
- **The framework**, if any — `grep -rlE 'django|fastapi|flask|sqlalchemy' pyproject.toml setup.cfg requirements*.txt`,
  or the presence of `manage.py`, `settings.py`, an `app = FastAPI()`. If one is
  found, load `references/web-framework-signatures.md` alongside the base
  signatures in §3; it adds nine signatures and a fourth behaviour class that
  the base file deliberately does not carry. Skip it for a library or a CLI —
  it is dead weight there.
- **The test suite's shape** — how many tests, how fast, what they assert on.
  This is the single most important input: the refactor plan's stop rule is
  "the suite is the contract", which is only sound if the suite is meaningful.
  If coverage of the modules you are about to change is thin, say so in the
  plan and make "add the characterization test first" the phase's step 1.

## 2. Measure, then read

Measurement narrows where to read. It does not produce findings on its own —
every metric below has a legitimate reason to be high, and shipping the metric
as the finding is the classic failure of this kind of review.

Run analysis tools **ephemerally** (`uvx <tool>`, `pipx run <tool>`) so the
review never adds a dependency. If the repo already has a tool configured, use
its configuration rather than your own flags.

```bash
# size and shape — the first cut
find . -name '*.py' -not -path './.venv/*' -not -path '*/node_modules/*' \
  | xargs wc -l | sort -rn | head -40

# complexity / arity / length signals (ruff needs no config for an ad-hoc select)
uvx ruff check --select C901,PLR0911,PLR0912,PLR0913,PLR0915 --statistics .

# simplification, dead code, redundancy signals
uvx ruff check --select SIM,RET,ARG,ERA,PIE,PERF,FURB,TRY,B --statistics .

# maintainability index + per-function complexity, ranked
uvx radon mi -s -o SCORE . | head -30
uvx radon cc -s -a -o SCORE . | head -40

# web repos only: Django idiom and event-loop-blocking signals
uvx ruff check --select DJ,ASYNC --statistics .

# genuinely unreferenced code (expect false positives on dynamic dispatch —
# heavily so in a web repo: views named as strings in urls.py, signal
# receivers, model methods used only from templates)
uvx vulture . --min-confidence 80

# churn: files changed most often are where comprehension debt costs most
git log --format=format: --name-only --since='18 months ago' \
  | grep '\.py$' | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn | head -25
```

Then **read every module in the tree**, ranked by the above. Reading is not
optional and cannot be replaced by the metrics — the highest-value findings in
this kind of codebase (duplication across sibling classes, an abstraction with
one implementation, defensive code for impossible states) score *well* on every
metric above, because each individual function is short and simple. The
complexity is in the arrangement, not the functions.

For a tree over ~40 modules, fan out: {% if agent == "claude" %}one `Agent` (subagent_type
`Explore`) per top-level package, each handed
`references/python-complexity-signatures.md` and asked to return findings in the
§3 shape{% else %}one pass per top-level package, carrying only
`references/python-complexity-signatures.md` and the §3 shape into each, so a
long tree does not blur the signatures{% endif %}. Fan-out is for *coverage*; you still read the
modules that come back with the heaviest findings yourself before writing them
into the plan.

## 3. Classify each finding

Load `references/python-complexity-signatures.md` for what to look for — it is
the substance of this skill, organized by the specific ways model-written
Python accretes complexity. Its last two sections are the ones that keep the
list honest: *What is not a finding* suppresses the false positives, and *When
the rule points the other way* covers the cases where a rule this skill leans on
is wrong for the module in front of you. Read both before you fix the act/park
line — most of the judgment in this review is there, not in the signatures.

If §1 found a web framework, load `references/web-framework-signatures.md` too
and treat its behaviour-class section as overriding §4's ordering.

Record each finding in this shape (`references/finding-shape.md` has the full
template and a worked example):

- **id** — phase letter + number, assigned in §4 (`B2`). This is the handle
  `--fix` takes, so it is stable for the life of the finding: a re-run carries
  an existing finding's id forward unchanged and only mints new ids for
  genuinely new findings, even when that leaves the letters out of order.
- **status** — `open` or `done` (with the date it was done). New findings are
  `open`; §6 is what flips one to `done`.
- **module and current size** — `core/doctor.py`, 436 lines. Concrete numbers,
  and a target where one is meaningful. "~280 lines with the decision logic
  visible" is checkable; "simpler" is not.
- **the finding** — what is duplicated, over-general, or unreadable, stated as
  a fact about the code, not a judgment.
- **the fix** — specific enough to execute, with the code sketch if the shape
  is not obvious from a sentence.
- **behaviour class** — `preserving`, `changes-output`, or `adds-surface`.
  Decide this by argument, not by hope, and write the argument down. "Both
  bodies are byte-identical, so lifting one to the base is preserving" is an
  argument. "This should be safe" is not.
- **the rejected alternative** — the more thorough variant you are not taking,
  and why. Also the *wrong* fix a reader might reach for, pre-empted.
- **tests affected** — for preserving findings, which suite proves it. For
  output-changing ones, which assertions must change; those changed assertions
  are the deliverable, so name them.
- **docs affected** — modules listed in an API reference, a nav file, an
  architecture page describing the contract you are changing.

**Then decide what not to do.** Every module you read that needs nothing gets
one line on the **fine** list. Every module that needs something you are
deliberately not doing now gets a line on the **park** list *with the reason*.
Keep the running list as you read — §5.1 requires one line for *every* module
read, and reconstructing it at write-up time is how modules go missing.
Typical park reasons that are correct: the module is subtle and currently
correct (merge engines, parsers, concurrency); the fix changes signatures
across half the callers for a readability gain; the fix is right but wants its
own test work first. A park list entry is a decision, not a TODO.

## 4. Phase the findings

Rules in full: `references/phasing-rules.md`. In short:

- Group by behaviour class first, then by module. Order **preserving →
  changes-output → adds-surface**. Within preserving, order by how much later
  phases depend on the result.
- One phase, one commit, one green gate. Each phase must be independently
  revertable and must end at the repo's own full validation command.
- Give each phase an explicit **behaviour contract sentence**. For preserving
  phases the sentence is: *the test suite passes unchanged; a test that needs a
  changed assertion is a signal the refactor changed behaviour — stop and
  report rather than updating the assertion.* This one rule is what makes a
  large refactor safe to hand to another session, and it must appear verbatim
  in the plan.
- Cap it. Five phases is a plan; twelve is a wish list that will be abandoned
  at phase three. If you have twelve, the bottom seven are a park list.
- Re-state the repo conventions that survive the refactor (typing strictness,
  the command entrypoint, test conventions the plan must not violate), so the
  executing session does not have to re-derive them from `CLAUDE.md`.

## 5. Write the plan, after showing it

Summarize to the user first, on one screen: findings per module, the phase
breakdown with behaviour class, and the park list. Ask for a go/no-go and for
whether to trim any phase — the park/act line is a judgment about their
priorities, not yours. {% if agent == "claude" %}Use `AskUserQuestion` if a real fork exists; otherwise
state the split in a few lines and proceed.{% else %}If a real fork exists, ask it as a
numbered question and wait; otherwise state the split in a few lines and proceed.{% endif %}

### 5.1 Write the review to the repo's review output directory

The review is a work product, not a design decision. It lands where the
developer can read, keep, or discard it without the repo's design record moving
underneath them. **Resolve the output directory, first hit wins:**

| Condition | Output directory |
|---|---|
| the invocation named a path | that path |
| `.rn-forge/agentkit/` exists (an agentkit-managed repo) | `.rn-forge/agentkit/output/python-simplify/` |
| `docs/reviews/` already exists | `docs/reviews/` |
| otherwise | `.python-simplify/` at the repo root |

Create the directory if it is missing, but **never stand up a `docs/` tree that
is not already there** — a review is not a reason to start a docs site. If the
repo's `.gitignore` carries a generated block (agentkit writes one), do not
hand-edit it to cover the output path; leave it alone and say so in the summary.

Write `complexity-review-<YYYY-MM-DD>.md`, self-contained, with its own heading
numbering — it does not inherit a host document's section numbers. It carries,
in order:

1. **Origin and date** — what was swept, what was deliberately not swept, why.
2. **Behaviour summary** — which phases are preserving and which are not.
3. **The phases**, in §4's order, each with its behaviour contract sentence.
4. **Verdicts** — *every module read gets exactly one line*, marked `act` (with
   the finding id), `park` (with the reason), or `fine`. A complete list, one
   line per module, not a prose paragraph naming the interesting few. A sweep
   that omits a module it read has failed §3 no matter how good its phases are,
   and the next run cannot converge because it has no way to tell "read and
   fine" from "never read".
5. **Carried forward** — what a prior review already settled (§0.1): findings
   done, verdicts inherited unchanged, and the date they came from.

Every finding heading carries its id and status: `### B2 — collapse the two
loader branches (open)`. That line is what `--fix B2` matches and what §6
rewrites on success.

### 5.2 Write the context file

Alongside the review, write `context.md` — the orientation §1 produced, so the
next run does not rediscover it. Nothing else: **no findings, no verdicts, no
plan.** Keeping it to orientation is what makes "delete the directory and
re-run" (§0.1) a clean reset rather than a way to lose decisions.

It records: the design record's location (including "asked the user, they said
none"), the repo's format/lint/test/validate commands, the framework detected,
the test suite's shape and where coverage is thin, and the handful of files
worth reading first in this repo. Head it with the date and the commit SHA it
was written from.

**Treat it as a cache, not as truth.** Before relying on any path it names,
check the path still exists; before trusting a command, check it is still in the
`Taskfile.yml`/`Makefile`/`pyproject.toml`. Anything stale gets re-derived and
the file rewritten — never quietly worked around. If the recorded SHA is
hundreds of commits behind, re-do §1 outright; it is cheap.

### 5.3 Promote into the design record only when asked

Whether a finding earns a permanent place in the repo's decision history is the
developer's call, not this skill's. After §5.1, offer promotion in one line and
stop there unless they take it. Promote into the location `context.md` records
— if that is "none", there is nothing to promote into. {% if agent == "claude" %}`AskUserQuestion` is the right shape for the
offer.{% else %}Ask it as a numbered question and wait.{% endif %}

On a yes, and only then:

| Repo has | Promote as |
|---|---|
| a numbered design record (`docs/specs/*.md` with `## N.` sections) | a new top-level section, numbered in sequence, **appended** |
| `docs/adr/` | a new ADR, in that directory's existing format |
| neither | nothing to promote into — the §5.1 file *is* the record; link it from `CLAUDE.md`/`AGENTS.md` instead |

Match the surrounding document's voice and heading depth exactly, and **append
only**: do not rewrite surrounding prose, renumber neighbouring sections, or
revise a framing paragraph so it mentions the new one. At most add a single
pointer line where the document already points at its other sections. An
appended section reviews as a clean diff; edits threaded through a document
somebody else wrote do not.

If the promotion is a new *page* rather than a section in an existing one, and
the docs build has a nav file (`mkdocs.yml`), add it there or the build fails it
as an orphan.

## 6. Execute and fix modes

Same procedure, different scope: `--execute <phase>` does one whole phase,
`--fix <id>` does one finding. **One of either per invocation. Never two.**

1. Find the plan — the path given in the invocation, else the newest
   `complexity-review-*.md` in the directory §5.1 resolves, else the design
   record section a promotion (§5.3) created. Read the target's section and the
   conventions it re-states. If the id or phase is already marked `done`, stop
   and say so rather than doing it twice.

   For `--fix`, check the ordering §4 gave it first: if the finding sits in a
   later phase and an earlier preserving phase it builds on is still `open`,
   say so and let the user choose — doing a `changes-output` finding while the
   preserving groundwork underneath it is unbuilt is how a "small fix" turns
   into an unreviewable diff. A single finding still gets the full gate and the
   full behaviour contract; `--fix` narrows the scope, not the rigour.
2. Confirm the working tree is clean and the gate is green **before** starting.
   A pre-existing failure discovered mid-refactor is indistinguishable from one
   you caused.
3. Make the changes, including the docs the phase names.
4. Run the repo's format, then its full validation command.
5. **Apply the phase's behaviour contract.** On a preserving phase, if a test
   fails, do not touch the assertion. Diagnose whether the refactor changed
   behaviour; if it did, revert and report. This is the rule the whole plan
   rests on — a session that "fixes" an assertion to get green has silently
   converted a safe refactor into an unreviewed behaviour change.
6. **Mark it done in the review**: flip the finding's (or every finding in the
   phase's) status to `done` with today's date, in the `complexity-review-*.md`
   the work came from. Leave everything else in that document untouched. This
   is the only edit §6 makes to the review, and it is what lets §0.1 build on
   the sweep instead of re-proposing work that has shipped.
7. Report: what changed, the gate result, and any finding the work surfaced
   that belongs in a later phase. Do not fold a newly-found improvement into
   the current phase; add it to the plan as a new `open` finding with a fresh
   id.

Do not commit or push unless asked.
